Description: antinomie: debug AArch32 programs on arm64, and refuse launches the engine cannot debug
 The arm64 debug engine refused AArch32 tasks. It now debugs them with
 their compat register sets (user_aarch32_ptrace_view: NT_PRSTATUS of r0-r15,
 cpsr and orig_r0, NT_ARM_VFP, NT_ARM_TLS; REGS_ALL reads that table), the
 same hardware step, and the arm64 kernel's AArch32 break instructions as
 software breakpoints: length 4 is A32 0xe7f001f0, length 2 Thumb 0xde01.
 arch/arm64/kernel/debug-monitors.c's try_handle_aarch32_break(), which
 do_el0_undef() calls for them, hands the trap to the engine first; it
 claims it only where it planted one, so a program's own break keeps its
 SIGTRAP. Each breakpoint keeps the instruction set it was planted for. A
 stepped SVC of either instruction set ends at the system call's exit. A
 Thumb breakpoint inside an IT block is not refused: whether it traps when
 the block's condition fails is the CPU's choice. DEBUG_CAPS offers
 ANTC_DEBUG_CAP_AARCH32.
 .
 A debug-on-start session whose launched image the engine cannot debug (an
 ia32 or x32 program on x86-64) used to stop it at its entry and leave the
 host attached to a program it could not read. Its entry stop is now not
 taken, the program runs on, and ATTACH (ADOPT) of the session, or any
 later command of it, answers EOPNOTSUPP and retires it; it never blocks a
 new session.
 .
 Contract revision 21.
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-04

